(We model Bihar as a dual economy with rural agriculture and urban non-farm sectors.) Tj
T* () Tj
T* (\\subsection{Production}) Tj
T* (\\begin{align}) Tj
T* (Y_a &= A_a K_a^{\\alpha_a}\(L_a \\bar{z}\)^{1-\\alpha_a},\\\\) Tj
T* (Y_u &= A_u K_u^{\\alpha_u}L_u^{1-\\alpha_u}.) Tj
T* (\\end{align}) Tj
T* ($\\bar{z}$ captures land-adjusted efficiency. Rural labor supply obeys $L_a+L_u=L$.) Tj
T* () Tj
T* (\\subsection{Migration decision under friction}) Tj
T* (Workers migrate when expected utility difference exceeds cost:) Tj
T* (\\begin{equation}) Tj
T* (E[w_u] - w_a - c_m\(\\theta,\\phi\) > 0,) Tj
T* (\\end{equation}) Tj
T* (where $c_m$ depends on social networks $\\theta$ \(reducing costs\) and urban congestion or) Tj
T* (informality risk $\\phi$ \(raising costs\). Bihar's existing migration networks lower transition) Tj
T* (costs, but thin local urban labor demand limits productive absorption.) Tj
T* () Tj
T* (\\subsection{Lewis turning point interpretation}) Tj
T* (The classic Lewis turning point is reached when surplus labor is exhausted and rural wages) Tj
T* (rise structurally. Bihar is unlikely near this threshold statewide, but localized turning) Tj
T* (points may emerge in better-connected districts. Policy should therefore target) Tj
T* (district-specific transitions, not one-size-fits-all state averages.) Tj
T* () Tj
T* (\\section{Analytical Framework IV: Public-Capital-Augmented Endogenous Growth}) Tj
T* (We incorporate public capital quality into an AK-style framework with externalities:) Tj
T* (\\begin{equation}) Tj
T* (Y_t = A_t K_t^{\\alpha}\(G_t^e\)^{\\gamma}\(H_t L_t\)^{1-\\alpha},) Tj
T* (\\end{equation}) Tj
T* (where $G_t^e = \\eta_t G_t$ is efficiency-adjusted public capital and $\\eta_t \\in \(0,1\)$) Tj
T* (measures execution quality.) Tj
T* () Tj
T* (TFP evolves as:) Tj
T* (\\begin{equation}) Tj
T* (\\ln A_{t+1} = \\rho \\ln A_t + \\psi_1 \\ln \\text{Connectivity}_t + \\psi_2 \\ln) Tj
T* (\\text{LearningQuality}_t + \\psi_3 \\ln \\text{FirmCapability}_t - \\psi_4 \\text{ClimateShock}_t +) Tj
T* (\\varepsilon_t.) Tj
T* (\\end{equation}) Tj
T* () Tj
T* (This equation formalizes a key argument: roads, schools, and irrigation matter not only) Tj
T* (directly but by raising future productivity through diffusion and market access.) Tj
T* () Tj
T* (\\section{Analytical Framework V: A Climate-Risk DSGE Sketch for Bihar}) Tj
T* (To connect macro stabilization with long-run development, consider a small open state-economy) Tj
T* (DSGE representation.) Tj
T* () Tj
T* (Representative household utility:) Tj
T* (\\begin{equation}) Tj
T* (U = E_0\\sum_{t=0}^{\\infty}\\beta^t\\left[\\frac{C_t^{1-\\sigma}}{1-\\sigma} -) Tj
T* (\\chi\\frac{N_t^{1+\\varphi}}{1+\\varphi}\\right].) Tj
T* (\\end{equation}) Tj
T* () Tj
T* (Budget constraint:) Tj
T* (\\begin{equation}) Tj
T* (C_t + I_t + B_{t+1} = W_tN_t + R_tK_t + \(1+r_t\)B_t - T_t + \\Pi_t + \\text{Remit}_t.) Tj
T* (\\end{equation}) Tj
T* () Tj
T* (Capital accumulation with climate damage:) Tj
T* (\\begin{equation}) Tj
T* (K_{t+1}=\(1-\\delta-\\omega \\mathcal{D}_t\)K_t+I_t,) Tj
T* (\\end{equation}) Tj
T* (where $\\mathcal{D}_t$ captures flood/heat damage shocks.) Tj
